recipe meshgen: build a mesh in code for an editor project

In an editor project, each `assets/gen/<name>.j3` is a small program that builds a mesh
and saves `assets/gen/<name>.j3mesh`. `j3c -e gen` runs the scripts that changed, and
render, play, and build run it too. Use the result as a mesh asset:
`j3c -e scene add mesh name=Tree asset=assets/gen/tree.j3mesh`.

- A script reruns when it or j3c changed. It does not see changes in other project files
  it imports; `j3c -e gen --force` reruns everything.
- Coordinates are meters, y up; `push`/`pop` save the transform, `color` sets vertex color.
- Next: `j3c doc j3.gfx.meshgen --short`.

==> assets/gen/tree.j3 <==
// A low-poly tree built in code: a tapered trunk and three cones of leaves.
// `j3c -e gen` (and every build, render, and play) runs it when it changes and writes
// assets/gen/tree.j3mesh. `j3c doc j3.gfx.meshgen` lists the shapes.
module gen_tree;

use j3.memory.Arena;
use j3.gfx.meshgen;
use j3.gfx.meshgen.Builder;

fn main() {
    let mem = Arena.create(4 * 1024 * 1024);
    let b = Builder.new(&mem);
    b.color(0.45, 0.3, 0.18);
    b.push();
    b.at(0, 0.6, 0);
    b.frustum(0.16, 0.1, 1.2, 10);
    b.pop();
    b.color(0.2, 0.55, 0.25);
    let tier = 0;
    while tier < 3 {
        b.push();
        b.at(0, 1.3 + f32(tier) * 0.55, 0);
        b.cone(0.8 - f32(tier) * 0.2, 1.0, 12);
        b.pop();
        tier += 1;
    }
    b.save(meshgen.output("assets/gen/tree.j3mesh"));
}
